1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,830
At the college fest, Aarav walks
across the stage with a microphone.

2
00:00:04,130 --> 00:00:05,749
He steps near the speaker.

3
00:00:05,749 --> 00:00:08,129
Suddenly, a loud scream fills the hall.

4
00:00:08,429 --> 00:00:10,923
He steps back, and the scream stops.

5
00:00:10,923 --> 00:00:12,261
What just happened?

6
00:00:12,561 --> 00:00:15,419
The speaker's sound looped
back into the microphone.

7
00:00:15,419 --> 00:00:17,161
That loop is called feedback.

8
00:00:17,661 --> 00:00:21,123
Feedback means sending part of
the output back to the input.

9
00:00:21,473 --> 00:00:24,092
Think of riding a bicycle
in a straight line.

10
00:00:24,442 --> 00:00:27,559
If the bike drifts left,
you steer a little right.

11
00:00:27,909 --> 00:00:30,026
The correction fights the change.

12
00:00:30,026 --> 00:00:31,749
This is negative feedback.

13
00:00:31,749 --> 00:00:33,328
It keeps things steady.

14
00:00:33,978 --> 00:00:36,409
Now think of a crowd at a cricket match.

15
00:00:36,759 --> 00:00:38,045
A few people cheer.

16
00:00:38,045 --> 00:00:40,519
Others hear them, and cheer even louder.

17
00:00:40,869 --> 00:00:43,600
The change feeds itself and keeps growing.

18
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,365
This is positive feedback.

19
00:00:46,015 --> 00:00:47,932
Let's build it on the breadboard.

20
00:00:48,282 --> 00:00:50,363
Here is an amplifier with gain A.

21
00:00:50,363 --> 00:00:52,534
It makes the signal A times bigger.

22
00:00:52,884 --> 00:00:55,884
Two resistors take a small part
of the output back to the start.

23
00:00:56,234 --> 00:01:00,021
That fraction is called the
feedback ratio, written beta.

24
00:01:00,371 --> 00:01:02,148
This circle is the mixer.

25
00:01:02,148 --> 00:01:04,585
It joins the input and the feedback.

26
00:01:04,935 --> 00:01:06,294
First, negative feedback.

27
00:01:06,294 --> 00:01:08,408
The feedback is subtracted from the input.

28
00:01:08,758 --> 00:01:11,808
The output becomes smaller,
but it stays clean and steady.

29
00:01:12,158 --> 00:01:14,615
Now we flip it to positive feedback.

30
00:01:14,615 --> 00:01:16,654
The feedback is added instead.

31
00:01:17,004 --> 00:01:18,687
The output grows bigger.

32
00:01:18,687 --> 00:01:20,688
We raise beta a little more.

33
00:01:21,038 --> 00:01:22,673
Now the loop feeds itself.

34
00:01:22,673 --> 00:01:24,713
The output hits the supply limits.

35
00:01:25,063 --> 00:01:27,755
It keeps going, even with no input at all.

36
00:01:27,755 --> 00:01:29,826
Just like the screaming microphone.

37
00:01:30,476 --> 00:01:33,166
Now the maths, one small step at a time.

38
00:01:33,516 --> 00:01:36,921
The amplifier sees the
input minus the feedback.

39
00:01:36,921 --> 00:01:39,184
That is V s minus beta V o.

40
00:01:39,884 --> 00:01:43,226
The output is A times
what the amplifier sees.

41
00:01:43,925 --> 00:01:47,979
So V o equals A, times V s minus beta V o.

42
00:01:48,679 --> 00:01:50,928
Bring the V o terms together.

43
00:01:50,928 --> 00:01:54,050
V o times one plus A beta equals AV s.

44
00:01:54,750 --> 00:01:59,072
So the gain with feedback, A f,
equals A over one plus A beta.

45
00:01:59,772 --> 00:02:01,458
A is the open loop gain.

46
00:02:01,458 --> 00:02:03,610
That is the gain without feedback.

47
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:06,933
For positive feedback, the sign flips.

48
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:09,534
A f equals A over one minus A beta.

49
00:02:10,234 --> 00:02:12,777
If A beta becomes one,
the bottom becomes zero.

50
00:02:12,777 --> 00:02:14,018
The circuit oscillates.

51
00:02:14,668 --> 00:02:16,958
Negative feedback calms the signal.

52
00:02:16,958 --> 00:02:18,790
Positive feedback feeds it.

53
00:02:19,590 --> 00:02:22,143
So which one is useful, and where?

54
00:02:22,493 --> 00:02:24,501
Negative feedback lowers the gain.

55
00:02:24,501 --> 00:02:26,795
But the gain becomes steady and clean.

56
00:02:27,145 --> 00:02:29,404
There is less distortion and less noise.

57
00:02:29,404 --> 00:02:30,969
So good amplifiers use it.

58
00:02:31,319 --> 00:02:33,471
Positive feedback raises the gain.

59
00:02:33,471 --> 00:02:35,574
But the circuit becomes unstable.

60
00:02:35,924 --> 00:02:39,840
That is bad for an amplifier,
but perfect for an oscillator.

61
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,047
An oscillator makes its own wave.

62
00:02:42,697 --> 00:02:43,858
Worked example.

63
00:02:43,858 --> 00:02:47,383
An amplifier has an open
loop gain of one thousand.

64
00:02:47,733 --> 00:02:52,084
We add negative feedback, with
beta equal to zero point zero one.

65
00:02:52,434 --> 00:02:56,301
First, A beta is one thousand
times zero point zero one.

66
00:02:56,301 --> 00:02:57,346
That is ten.

67
00:02:58,046 --> 00:03:01,449
A f equals one thousand,
over one plus ten.

68
00:03:02,149 --> 00:03:04,299
That is one thousand over eleven.

69
00:03:04,299 --> 00:03:05,517
About ninety one.

70
00:03:06,217 --> 00:03:11,343
Now positive feedback, with beta equal
to zero point zero zero zero five.

71
00:03:12,043 --> 00:03:14,069
A beta is zero point five.

72
00:03:14,069 --> 00:03:17,293
A f equals one thousand
over zero point five.

73
00:03:17,993 --> 00:03:19,556
That is two thousand.

74
00:03:19,556 --> 00:03:21,686
Negative feedback cut the gain.

75
00:03:21,686 --> 00:03:23,654
Positive feedback doubled it.

76
00:03:24,304 --> 00:03:25,766
Now, the exam trap.

77
00:03:26,116 --> 00:03:29,518
Many students write one minus
A beta for negative feedback.

78
00:03:29,868 --> 00:03:30,768
Wrong.

79
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,165
Negative feedback has one plus A beta.

80
00:03:33,165 --> 00:03:35,031
So the gain always falls.

81
00:03:35,681 --> 00:03:37,750
And beta here is the feedback ratio.

82
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:39,944
It is not the transistor's current gain.

83
00:03:40,294 --> 00:03:41,915
Where is this asked?

84
00:03:41,915 --> 00:03:43,775
In PUC board papers.

85
00:03:44,425 --> 00:03:49,339
Define feedback, draw the block diagram,
derive the gain, and solve small

86
00:03:49,339 --> 00:03:50,239
problems.

87
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:51,680
Try this.

88
00:03:51,611 --> 00:03:55,007
An amplifier has an open
loop gain of two hundred.

89
00:03:55,007 --> 00:03:59,123
Negative feedback with beta zero
point zero four five is added.

90
00:03:59,123 --> 00:04:00,745
What is the new gain?

91
00:04:06,145 --> 00:04:07,045
C.

92
00:04:06,577 --> 00:04:07,477
Twenty.

93
00:04:07,623 --> 00:04:11,342
A beta is two hundred times
zero point zero four five.

94
00:04:11,342 --> 00:04:12,455
That is nine.

95
00:04:12,805 --> 00:04:16,056
A f equals two hundred,
over one plus nine.

96
00:04:16,406 --> 00:04:18,583
Two hundred over ten is twenty.

97
00:04:18,583 --> 00:04:21,577
The gain falls, as
negative feedback should.

98
00:04:22,227 --> 00:04:23,773
Screenshot this card.

99
00:04:26,973 --> 00:04:29,152
Next, the effects of negative feedback.

100
00:04:29,152 --> 00:04:31,256
Why do engineers happily give up gain?
